00:58:18,990 --> 00:58:21,290
不再是单个神经元对应一个概念
it's not just like a single neuron that corresponds to a concept.

1356
00:58:21,590 --> 00:58:23,930
单个神经元可能对应十几个概念
A single neuron might correspond to a dozen concepts.

1357
00:58:24,490 --> 00:58:26,710
如果它与其他神经元一起被激活
And if it's activated together with other neurons,

1358
00:58:26,770 --> 00:58:27,830
这被称为叠加
this is called superposition.

1359
00:58:28,570 --> 00:58:31,970
它们共同表示一个更复杂的概念
And together, it represents this more sophisticated concept.

1360
00:58:32,590 --> 00:58:34,450
而这正是我们一直在持续学习的内容
And it's just something we're learning about all the time.

1361
00:58:35,130 --> 00:58:39,030
你知道的，对 Anthropic 来说，当我们思考这个领域将如何发展时
You know, for Anthropic, as we think about the way this space evolves,

1362
00:58:39,990 --> 00:58:43,370
以一种对世界安全且有益的方式来做这件事
doing this in a way that is safe and good for the world is just,

1363
00:58:43,470 --> 00:58:44,730
正是我们存在的理由
this is the reason that we exist.

1364
00:58:45,130 --> 00:58:47,290
也是每个人选择加入 Anthropic 的原因
And this is the reason that everyone is at Anthropic.

1365
00:58:47,890 --> 00:58:48,670
在这里的每一个人
Everyone that is here,

1366
00:58:48,770 --> 00:58:49,850
他们在这里的理由都是这个
this is the reason why they're here.

1367
00:58:50,370 --> 00:58:51,530
所以我们在这方面的很多工作
So a lot of this work,

1368
00:58:51,590 --> 00:58:52,350
实际上是开源的
we actually open source.

1369
00:58:52,690 --> 00:58:53,810
我们频繁发表相关成果
We publish it a lot.

1370
00:58:54,530 --> 00:58:56,910
而且我们非常开放地发表这些内容来讨论这件事
And, you know, we publish very freely to talk about this,

1371
00:58:57,230 --> 00:59:02,130
只是为了激励其他在做类似事情的实验室 也能以安全的方式来进行
just so we can inspire other labs that are working on similar things to do it in a way that's safe.

1372
00:59:02,950 --> 00:59:05,210
这同样是我们在 Claude Code 上一直在做的事情
And this is something that we've been doing for Claude Code also.

1373
00:59:05,330 --> 00:59:08,550
我们在内部称之为通往高标准的竞赛
We call this the race to the top internally.

1374
00:59:08,550 --> 00:59:12,370
比如说在 Claude Code 上，我们就发布了一个开源沙盒
And so for Claude Code, for example, we released an open source sandbox.

1375
00:59:12,930 --> 00:59:16,130
这是一个你可以在其中运行智能体的沙盒
And this is a sandbox that you can run the agent in.

1376
00:59:16,730 --> 00:59:20,110
它可以确保存在一定的边界 让智能体无法访问你系统上的所有内容
And it just makes sure that there are certain boundaries and it can't access everything on your system.

1377
00:59:20,970 --> 00:59:22,190
而且我们把它开源了
And we made that open source.

1378
00:59:22,590 --> 00:59:23,930
它实际上可以与任何智能体配合使用
And it actually works with any agent,

1379
00:59:24,090 --> 00:59:28,170
不仅仅是 Claude Code 因为我们希望让其他人也能很容易地做到同样的事情
not just Claude Code, because we wanted to make it really easy for others to do the same thing.

1380
00:59:28,950 --> 00:59:30,350
所以这只是同样的原则
So this is just the same principle,

1381
00:59:30,490 --> 00:59:31,390
争相冲到顶端
race to the top.

1382
00:59:31,730 --> 00:59:33,830
我们想要确保这件事顺利进行
We want to make sure this thing goes well.

1383
00:59:34,090 --> 00:59:36,290
而这只是我们手里的一根杠杆
And this is just the lever that we have.

1384
00:59:36,990 --> 00:59:39,650
太不可思议了 好的 我肯定想在这上面多花点时间
Incredible. Okay. I definitely want to spend more time on that.

1385
00:59:39,870 --> 00:59:41,350
我会跟进这个建议
I will follow up with this suggestion.

1386
00:59:41,870 --> 00:59:46,750
还有一件我在一线从工程师那里注意到的事情
Something else that I've been noticing in the field across engineers,

1387
00:59:46,870 --> 00:59:48,630
从产品经理以及其他和智能体一起工作的人那里注意到的事情
product managers, others that work with agents,

1388
00:59:49,150 --> 00:59:53,910
就是当他们的智能体不工作时 人们会有一种焦虑感
is there's this kind of anxiety people feel when their agents aren't working.

1389
00:59:54,650 --> 00:59:55,830
会有一种感觉
There's a sense that like,

1390
00:59:55,870 --> 00:59:58,190
哦天哪 Nisa 有个问题等着我回答
oh, man, Nisa has a question I need to answer,

1391
00:59:58,390 --> 00:59:59,610
或者它被什么东西卡住了
or it's like blocked on something,

1392
00:59:59,890 --> 01:00:03,510
或者我正在损失大量的生产力
or there's all this productivity I'm losing.

1393
01:00:03,690 --> 01:00:05,790
我得赶紧醒来把它重新跑起来
I need to wake up and get it going again.

1394
01:00:06,250 --> 01:00:07,030
这是你会有的感觉吗
Is that something you feel?

1395
01:00:07,030 --> 01:00:08,210
你的团队也会有这种感觉吗
Is that something your team feels?

1396
01:00:08,350 --> 01:00:11,310
你觉得这是一个我们需要跟踪并认真思考的问题吗
Do you feel like this is a problem we need to track and think about?

1397
01:00:11,670 --> 01:00:12,970
我总是让一堆智能体在跑
I always have a bunch of agents running.

1398
01:00:13,150 --> 01:00:15,310
比如说此刻我大概有五个智能体在跑
So like at the moment I have like five agents running.

1399
01:00:15,710 --> 01:00:16,750
而且在任何时候
And at any moment, like,

1400
01:00:16,830 --> 01:00:19,250
你知道 就是我一醒来就会启动一堆智能体
you know, like I wake up and I start a bunch of agents.

1401
01:00:19,770 --> 01:00:21,290
比如我今天早上醒来做的第一件事就是
Like the first thing I did when I woke up was like,

1402
01:00:21,350 --> 01:00:23,170
哦天哪 我特别想检查一下这件事
oh man, I really want to check this thing.

1403
01:00:23,270 --> 01:00:24,330
所以我就拿起手机
So like I opened up my phone,

1404
01:00:24,510 --> 01:00:26,890
打开 Claude iOS 应用 切到 Code 标签页
Claude iOS app, Code tab,

1405
01:00:27,010 --> 01:00:29,170
然后就像 智能体 帮我做什么什么 那样
you know, like agent do blah, blah, blah.

1406
01:00:29,630 --> 01:00:31,350
因为我昨天写了一些代码 然后我在想
Because I wrote some code yesterday and I was like,

1407
01:00:31,410 --> 01:00:32,490
等等 我这个写得对吗
wait, did I do this right?

1408
01:00:32,550 --> 01:00:35,570
我有点在反复怀疑某个地方 结果发现其实是对的
I was like, kind of double guessing something and it was correct.

1409
01:00:36,070 --> 01:00:37,690
但现在做这种事实在太容易了
But now it's just like so easy to do this.

1410
01:00:38,630 --> 01:00:41,350
所以我也说不清 可能是会有一点点焦虑
So I don't know, there is this little bit of anxiety maybe.

1411
01:00:41,810 --> 01:00:44,850
我个人其实没太感受到 因为我一直都有智能体在跑
I personally haven't really felt it just because I have agents running all the time.

1412
01:00:45,610 --> 01:00:48,050
而且我也不再被锁死在终端里了
And I'm also just like not locked into a terminal anymore.

1413
01:00:48,510 --> 01:00:50,250
现在大概有三分之一的代码是在终端里写的
Maybe a third of my code now is in the terminal,

1414
01:00:50,430 --> 01:00:53,230
但还有三分之一是用桌面应用写的
but also a third is using the desktop app.

1415
01:00:53,630 --> 01:00:55,890
剩下三分之一是在 iOS 应用里写的
And then a third is the iOS app,

1416
01:00:55,910 --> 01:01:02,390
这太让人惊讶了 因为我从没想过哪怕到了 2026 年 我会用这种方式写代码
which is just so surprising because I did not think that would be the way that I code in even in 2026.

1417
01:01:02,670 --> 01:01:04,750
我很喜欢你还把这称作写代码
I love that you describe it as coding still,

1418
01:01:04,890 --> 01:01:08,210
虽然本质上只是和 Claude Code 对话 让它替你写代码
which is just talking to the Claude Code to code for you essentially.

1419
01:01:08,590 --> 01:01:10,950
而有趣的是 这现在就被叫作写代码
And it's interesting that this is now coding.

1420
01:01:11,310 --> 01:01:13,730
现在写代码就是描述你想要什么
Coding now is describing what you want,

1421
01:01:13,810 --> 01:01:15,210
而不是亲自写具体的代码
not writing actual code.

1422
01:01:16,050 --> 01:01:20,690
我有点好奇 以前用穿孔卡片之类东西写代码的人
I kind of wonder if the people that used to code using punch cards or whatever,

1423
01:01:20,830 --> 01:01:21,690
如果你给他们看软件
if you show them software,

1424
01:01:21,950 --> 01:01:22,750
他们会怎么评价
what they would have said.

1425
01:01:23,850 --> 01:01:25,270
我记得我读到过一些东西
I remember reading something,

1426
01:01:25,270 --> 01:01:30,530
可能是早期版本的 ACM 杂志之类的
maybe very early versions of ACM magazine or something,

1427
01:01:31,510 --> 01:01:32,190
里面有人在说
where people were saying,

1428
01:01:32,270 --> 01:01:33,070
不 这不是一回事
no, it's not the same thing.

1429
01:01:33,550 --> 01:01:34,770
这不是真正的写代码
This isn't really coding.

1430
01:01:35,910 --> 01:01:37,050
他们把那叫作编程
They called it programming.

1431
01:01:37,170 --> 01:01:38,490
我觉得 coding 这个词其实挺新的
I think coding is kind of a new word.

1432
01:01:39,590 --> 01:01:40,550
但我经常会这样想
But I kind of think about this.

1433
01:01:40,790 --> 01:01:43,030
追溯到过去 我家是从苏联来的
Back in the... my family is from the Soviet Union.

1434
01:01:43,310 --> 01:01:44,830
我出生在乌克兰
I was born in Ukraine.

1435
01:01:45,970 --> 01:01:48,850
我外公其实是苏联最早的一批程序员之一
And my grandpa was actually one of the first programmers in the Soviet Union.

1436
01:01:49,770 --> 01:01:51,870
他就是用穿孔卡片来编程的
And he programmed using punch cards.

1437
01:01:52,910 --> 01:01:55,270
然后你知道 他跟我妈说过
And, you know, he told... my mom,

1438
01:01:55,430 --> 01:01:59,330
我妈长大后会讲这些故事 或者说她会讲起她小时候
growing up, told these stories of like... or she told these stories of when she was

1439
01:01:59,330 --> 01:02:00,970
小时候 他会把那些穿孔卡片带回家
growing up, he would bring these punch cards home.

1440
01:02:01,350 --> 01:02:03,810
家里会有一大摞一大摞的穿孔卡片
And there was these like big stacks of punch cards.

1441
01:02:04,570 --> 01:02:06,650
对她来说 就是在上面拿蜡笔到处乱画
And for her, she would like draw all over them with crayons.

1442
01:02:06,730 --> 01:02:07,930
那就是她的童年记忆
And that was like her childhood memory.

1443
01:02:08,510 --> 01:02:10,270
但对他来说 那就是他的编程体验
But for him, that was like his experience of programming.

1444
01:02:10,390 --> 01:02:12,530
而他其实从没见证过向软件的转变
And he actually never saw the software transition.

1445
01:02:13,310 --> 01:02:15,050
但在某个时刻 这一切确实转变成了软件
But at some point it did transition to software.

1446
01:02:15,130 --> 01:02:19,690
我觉得可能有一代更老的程序员 并没有太把软件当回事
And I think there's probably this older generation of programmers that just didn't take software very seriously.

1447
01:02:20,130 --> 01:02:20,990
他们可能会说
And they would have been like,

1448
01:02:21,030 --> 01:02:22,370
嗯 你知道 这不是真正的写代码
well, you know, it's not really coding.

1449
01:02:22,830 --> 01:02:26,150
但我觉得这个领域一直以来就是以这种方式在变化
But I think this is a field that just has always been changing in this way.

1450
01:02:27,010 --> 01:02:27,970
我想你大概不知道
I don't think you know this,

1451
01:02:28,010 --> 01:02:29,350
其实我也是在乌克兰出生的
but I was born in Ukraine also.

1452
01:02:30,570 --> 01:02:31,330
哦 我不知道啊
Oh, I don't know that.

1453
01:02:31,470 --> 01:02:33,650
是啊 你是哪个城市的 我来自敖德萨
Yeah. Which town? I'm from Odessa.

1454
01:02:34,430 --> 01:02:34,970
哦 我也是
Oh, me too.

1455
01:02:36,670 --> 01:02:37,850
是啊 太疯狂了
Yeah, that's crazy.

1456
01:02:39,170 --> 01:02:41,170
哇 太不可思议了 多么巧合的时刻
Wow. Incredible. What a moment.

1457
01:02:41,650 --> 01:02:43,250
说不定我们还有点小小的渊源
Maybe related in some small way.

1458
01:02:44,170 --> 01:02:47,030
你和你的家人是哪一年离开的
What year did you leave and your family leave?

1459
01:02:48,430 --> 01:02:49,630
我们是 95 年来的
We came in 95.

1460
01:02:50,430 --> 01:02:52,210
好的 我们是 88 年离开的 稍微早一点
Okay. We left in 88, a little earlier.

1461
01:02:52,850 --> 01:02:56,130
是啊 要是当初没离开 人生会完全不一样 对吧
Yeah. What a different life that would have been to not leave, huh?

1462
01:02:57,090 --> 01:03:01,310
是的 我每天都觉得自己很幸运 能在这里长大
Yeah. I feel so lucky every day that I get to grow up here.

1463
01:03:02,170 --> 01:03:04,410
是啊 我家每次一碰杯 或吃饭的时候
Yeah. My family, anytime there's like a toast or a meal,

1464
01:03:04,550 --> 01:03:05,750
他们就会说 敬美国
they're just like, to America.

1465
01:03:07,810 --> 01:03:09,410
就会觉得 好了好了 这话说够了
It's like, okay, enough about that.

1466
01:03:09,430 --> 01:03:10,130
但你能理解的
But you get it, you know,

1467
01:03:10,150 --> 01:03:11,910
一旦你认真想过自己的人生本来可能会是什么样
once you start really thinking about what life could have been.

1468
01:03:12,810 --> 01:03:14,610
是 完全是这样 我们也会敬同样的酒
Yeah, exactly. Yeah. We do the same toast,

1469
01:03:14,710 --> 01:03:15,390
但喝的还是伏特加
but it's still vodka.

1470
01:03:16,230 --> 01:03:17,470
还是伏特加 绝对的
It's still vodka. Absolutely.

1471
01:03:19,370 --> 01:03:21,790
哦天哪 好的 那我再问你几件事
Oh, man. Okay. Let me ask you a couple more things here.

1472
01:03:22,350 --> 01:03:26,490
你分享了一些非常棒的技巧 关于如何最大化利用 AI
You shared some really cool tips for how to get the most out of AI,

1473
01:03:26,630 --> 01:03:28,850
如何基于 AI 构建 如何在 AI 之上打造优秀的产品
how to build on AI, how to build great products on AI.

1474
01:03:29,310 --> 01:03:32,530
你分享的一个建议是 给你的团队尽可能多的 tokens
One tip you shared is give your team as many tokens as they want.

1475
01:03:32,610 --> 01:03:33,690
就让他们尽情试验
Just let them experiment.

1476
01:03:34,310 --> 01:03:38,490
你还给了一个更通用的建议 就是要面向模型的发展方向去构建
You also shared just advice generally of just build towards where the model is going,

1477
01:03:38,490 --> 01:03:39,570
而不是停留在模型今天的能力上
not to where it is today.

1478
01:03:39,730 --> 01:03:42,290
对于那些正在尝试构建 AI 产品的人 你还有哪些建议
What other advice do you have for folks that are trying to build AI products?

1479
01:03:43,090 --> 01:03:44,030
我大概还会再分享几件事
I'd probably share a few more things.

1480
01:03:44,150 --> 01:03:46,390
其中一条是 不要试图把模型关进一个小盒子里
So one is don't try to box the model in.

1481
01:03:47,330 --> 01:03:51,810
我觉得很多人在基于模型构建东西时的本能反应是试图让模型
I think a lot of people's instinct when they build on the model is they try to make it

1482
01:03:51,810 --> 01:03:53,190
以一种非常特定的方式表现
behave a very particular way.

1483
01:03:53,710 --> 01:03:55,910
他们会想，这是一个更大系统里的一个组件
They're like, this is a component of a bigger system.

1484
01:03:56,050 --> 01:03:59,890
我觉得有些例子就是人们在模型外面套上非常严格的工作流
I think some examples of this are people wearing like very strict workflows on the model,

1485
01:03:59,950 --> 01:04:02,410
比如说，你知道，就好像在说，你必须先做第一步
for example, you know, to say like, you must do step one,

1486
01:04:02,490 --> 01:04:03,630
然后第二步，再第三步
then step two, then step three.

1487
01:04:03,650 --> 01:04:05,750
然后你会有一个非常花哨的编排器来做这些事
And you have this like very fancy orchestrator doing this.

1488
01:04:06,250 --> 01:04:09,170
但实际上几乎总是这样：如果你只是给模型一些工具
But actually almost always you get better results if you just give the model tools,

1489
01:04:09,270 --> 01:04:10,930
给它一个目标然后让它自己想办法完成你会得到更好的结果
you give it a goal and you let it figure it out.

1490
01:04:11,450 --> 01:04:14,130
我觉得一年前你确实需要很多这种脚手架
I think a year ago, you actually needed a lot of the scaffolding,

1491
01:04:14,210 --> 01:04:15,390
但现在其实已经不太需要了
but nowadays you don't really need it.

1492
01:04:16,030 --> 01:04:18,510
所以我也不知道该怎么称呼这个原则
So, you know, I don't know what to call this principle,

1493
01:04:18,650 --> 01:04:19,330
但大概就是那种
but it's like, you know,

1494
01:04:19,370 --> 01:04:21,190
有点像“不要问模型能为你做什么”
like ask not what the model can do for you.

1495
01:04:21,750 --> 01:04:23,070
大概是类似这样的意思
Maybe it's something like this.

1496
01:04:23,490 --> 01:04:26,390
你只需要思考如何给模型提供完成事情所需的工具
Just think about how do you give the model the tools to do things?

1497
01:04:26,470 --> 01:04:27,590
不要试图过度管控它
Don't try to over curate it.

1498
01:04:27,650 --> 01:04:28,850
不要试图把它关进一个小盒子里
Don't try to put it into a box.

1499
01:04:29,410 --> 01:04:31,210
不要一开始就塞给它一大堆上下文
Don't try to give it a bunch of context up front.

1500
01:04:31,430 --> 01:04:33,850
给它一个工具让它自己去获取所需的上下文
Give it a tool so that it can get the context it needs.

1501
01:04:34,210 --> 01:04:35,430
这样你就是会得到更好的结果
You're just going to get better results.

1502
01:04:37,140 --> 01:04:43,060
我觉得第二点其实是这个原则的一个更通用的版本
I think a second one is maybe actually like a more even more general version of this

1503
01:04:43,060 --> 01:04:44,800
就是所谓的“苦涩教训”
principle is just the bitter lesson.

1504
01:04:46,200 --> 01:04:47,640
实际上对Claude Code团队来说
